Make the JIT's unaligned memory accesses safe on targets that trap on them.

These are generic problems on any target that cannot do arbitrary unaligned
loads and stores; none of them is IA-64 specific (SPARC is affected the same
way). Measured on Itanium 2, an access faults only when it crosses an 8-byte
boundary, and the kernel's fixup handler costs thousands of cycles.

1. BaselineCodeGen.cpp -- the Baseline Interpreter's operand helpers read a
   2- or 4-byte operand at pc + sizeof(jsbytecode), which is by construction at
   an arbitrary alignment, so a 4-byte operand read trapped 3 times in 8. They
   now go through load32Unaligned() / load16UnalignedZeroExtend(), which
   already exist on every backend and are the same instruction on targets that
   do not care.

2. MacroAssembler.cpp -- iteratorMore() incremented NativeIterator's
   propertyCursor_, a uint32_t, with addPtr(). On little-endian that happens to
   yield the right value while also rewriting the adjacent word; on big-endian
   it targets the adjacent field instead, so the iterator never advances. It is
   simply a 32-bit field, so add32() is correct on either endianness and
   properly aligned. The old form straddled an 8-byte boundary on every for-in
   iteration.

3. MacroAssembler.cpp -- branchIfNotStringCharsEquals() compares a string
   against a constant in decreasing strides, and finishes an odd-length tail
   with one *overlapping* wider load ("exam" + "mple" rather than "exam" +
   "pl" + "e"), which starts mid-word by construction. Gate that on
   SupportsUnalignedAccesses(); without it the loop just carries on with the
   narrower strides, whose offsets are always naturally aligned.

4. CodeGenerator.cpp -- CopyStringChars() copies a pointer-sized block at a
   time when the source and destination encodings match, but |from| and |to|
   advance at character granularity, so those loads and stores land at
   arbitrary alignment. Gate the wide path on SupportsUnalignedAccesses(); the
   per-character fallback copies at the character width, which is always
   naturally aligned.

5. MacroAssembler.cpp -- the same function's *main* loop has the same problem
   in a less obvious form. Its offsets are multiples of the stride from
   |stringChars|, so they look aligned, but |stringChars| itself is only
   aligned to the character size: a Latin-1 string's chars are a plain byte
   pointer, and a dependent string of either encoding points into the middle
   of its base. Comparing two Latin-1 characters with one 2-byte load
   therefore traps whenever the string starts at 7 mod 8. Cap the stride at
   the character size unless the target supports unaligned accesses, and make
   compareStringChars()'s single-instruction path (which reads up to 8 bytes
   at offset 0) make the same distinction.

   Found on Octane/Typescript, whose scanner compares source text against the
   atom "///"; that is a 3-byte constant, so the loop emitted a 2-byte compare
   at +0 and a 1-byte compare at +2.

